  "tempo": 1.12,
  "segments": [
    {
      "text": "Your gas pump knows when your tank is full without talking to your car.",
      "start": 0.082,
      "end": 3.512
    },
    {
      "text": "Near the tip of the nozzle is a tiny sensing hole connected to an air passage.",
      "start": 3.88,
      "end": 7.825
    },
    {
      "text": "While you're pumping, air can move through it,",
      "start": 8.141,
      "end": 10.159
    },
    {
      "text": "but once the gasoline rises high enough to cover that hole, the airflow suddenly",
      "start": 10.435,
      "end": 14.363
    },
    {
      "text": "changes.",
      "start": 14.363,
      "end": 14.845
    },
    {
      "text": "That pressure change triggers a mechanical diaphragm inside the nozzle",
      "start": 15.157,
      "end": 18.48
    },
    {
      "text": "and",
      "start": 18.841,
      "end": 19.037
    },
    {
      "text": "click.",
      "start": 19.36,
      "end": 19.646
    },
    {
      "text": "The valve shuts.",
      "start": 19.915,
      "end": 20.7
    },
    {
      "text": "No Wi-Fi, no computer, no secret signal from your car.",
      "start": 21.062,
      "end": 24.436
    },
    {
      "text": "Just air pressure and a ridiculously clever little piece of mechanical",
      "start": 24.742,
      "end": 27.904
    },
    {
      "text": "engineering.",
      "start": 27.904,
      "end": 28.475
    }
  ],
